.Dd October 26, 2026
.Dt JJCLIENT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm jjclient
.Nd create blog entries
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl r
.Op Fl h Ar hostname
.Op Fl u Ar username
.Op Fl p Ar password
.Op Fl -password-fd Ar fd
.Op Fl s Ar subject
.Op Fl -date Ar timestamp
.Op Fl -security Ar public|friends|private
.Op Fl -format Ar text|markdown|html
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility creates blog entries on Just Journal based websites.
.Pp
The username must be set.
If neither password option is supplied, the password is read without echo
from the controlling terminal
.Pa /dev/tty .
This preserves standard input for the entry body.
If no controlling terminal is available, a password option is required.
If the hostname is not specified,
.Nm
will use the www.justjournal.com site.
.Pp
As of version 2.0.2, all requests require HTTPS.  Prior versions
used HTTP which is not secure.
.Pp
Version 2.1.0 uses the JSON REST API with session authentication.
The server must provide /api/login and /api/entry.
Session cookies are kept in memory and are not saved to disk.
.Pp
Input must be UTF-8 and fit within 31,999 bytes.
Larger entries are rejected.
.Pp
The following options are available:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl d
Debug login and posting problems.
.It Fl r
Print the date, subject, and body of the latest 20 entries for the authenticated
account, including private entries and drafts, instead of reading an entry
from standard input or posting it.
.It Fl h
Specify the host to connect to.
.It Fl u
Specify the username to use while connecting.
.It Fl p Ar password
Define the password used while authenticating.
This compatibility option can expose the password through process arguments
and shell history; prefer the terminal prompt or
.Fl -password-fd .
.It Fl -password-fd Ar fd
Read the password from the first line of file descriptor
.Ar fd ,
or through EOF if no newline is present.
The form
.Fl -password-fd Ns = Ns Ar fd
is also accepted.
A trailing carriage return is removed for CRLF input;
other spaces are preserved.
Passwords must contain 6 to 30 bytes.
Invalid descriptors and invalid or oversized passwords are rejected.
Descriptor 0 is supported: the first line is the password and the remaining
standard input is the entry body.
Use a separate descriptor to keep body input untouched.
This option and
.Fl p
are mutually exclusive.
.It Fl s
Set the subject (title) of the blog entry.
.It Fl -date Ar timestamp
Set the entry date using an ISO 8601 timestamp with seconds and an explicit
timezone, for example
.Li 2026-10-25T14:30:00-04:00
or
.Li 2026-10-25T18:30:00Z .
One to three fractional second digits are optional.
Calendar dates must be valid, with years from 0001 to 9999.
If omitted, the server uses the current time.
.It Fl -security Ar public|friends|private
Set entry visibility.
The default is
.Cm public .
.It Fl -format Ar text|markdown|html
Set the body format.
The default is
.Cm text .
Markdown and HTML bodies are sent unchanged for server rendering, with automatic
line-break formatting disabled.
Legacy extraction of
.Li <title>
and
.Li <music>
tags applies only to text mode.
.El
.Pp
The date, security and format options also accept
.Li --option=value
and cannot be combined with
.Fl r .
.Pp
Password input through environment variables is not supported.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Prompt for a password while reading the entry from a file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
jjclient -u username -s 'My title' < entry.txt
.Ed
.Pp
Read a password from a file readable only by the intended user:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
jjclient -u username --password-fd 3 < entry.txt 3< protected-password-file
.Ed
.Pp
Post a private HTML entry with a specified date:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
jjclient -u username --security private --format html \e
    --date '2026-10-25T18:30:00Z' < entry.html
.Ed
.Sh AUTHORS
The
.Nm 
utility was written by Lucas Holt <luke@foolishgames.com>
for JustJournal.com.
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
utility was written for MidnightBSD and later ported to FreeBSD,
Linux, and Mac OS X.
